{"id":64,"course_id":20,"title":"IAM Principals, Policies, and Evaluation","summary":"Understand who can do what and why AWS denied or allowed it.","sequence":1,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nIAM decisions combine identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, session policies, and explicit deny. Debugging starts by identifying the principal and action.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reading policy statements, actions, resources, conditions, and CloudTrail access denied events.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nExplain why a role with an apparent allow can still be denied by boundary, SCP, condition, or resource policy.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nIAM decisions combine identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, session policies, and explicit deny. Debugging starts by identifying the principal and action.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reading policy statements, actions, resources, conditions, and CloudTrail access denied events.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nExplain why a role with an apparent allow can still be denied by boundary, SCP, condition, or resource policy.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-aws-iam-for-eks","course_category":"AWS IAM","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":291,"simplified":"AWS IAM for EKS and Platform Teams","traditional":"AWS IAM for EKS and Platform Teams","pinyin":"AWS IAM","definition":"Learn IAM policies, roles, trust relationships, STS, IRSA, Pod Identity, and least-privilege reviews for EKS workloads."},{"id":292,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":293,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":294,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":295,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":175,"lesson_id":64,"prompt":"What is the main goal of IAM Principals, Policies, and Evaluation?","answer":"Understand who can do what and why AWS denied or allowed it.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":176,"lesson_id":64,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":177,"lesson_id":64,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
